# A program that must NOT compile, in two different ways, which is the whole
# point of it: `import threading` needs the thread-safe runtime, and that
# runtime exists on x86-64/i386/aarch64/arm32 and nowhere else.
#
#   where the locks exist   -> name the flag, the user can act on it
#   where they do not       -> say threading is absent, because prescribing
#                              --threadsafe there sends the user into the
#                              driver's own refusal of that same flag
#
# The Makefile row asserts both wordings, the driver's refusal of the flag on
# wasm32, and -- the arm that makes the other three mean anything -- that WITH
# the flag on the host this same file compiles and prints its line. Without
# that arm every refusal row would also pass on a fixture that simply does not
# compile. feature-n-import-threading-should-imply-threadsafe
import threading


lock = threading.Lock()
lock.acquire()
lock.release()
print("locked and unlocked")
